  :adr/body "## Coinbase Smart Wallet's approach is the one this app already implements

The instruction was to follow it. Measured, it is already followed, and that
answers the question the last three exchanges were circling.

`src/cloud/itonami/app/smart_account.cljk` reproduces the Coinbase Smart Wallet
1.1 factory's `getAddress(bytes[],uint256)` locally, for an account whose owner
is a raw 64-byte P-256 public key. `GET /api/wallet` on this install returns:

    wallet-provider        WebAuthn P-256 / ERC-4337
    private-keys-stored?   false
    principal-account      owner-kind  webauthn-p256
                           factory     coinbase-smart-wallet 1.1
                           entry point 0.6
                           deployment  not-yet-deployed
                           address     0x6160…5f39

And `web.clj` has been saying it on screen the whole time: 「Passkey が Smart
Account を所有し、Bot は送金内容を提案します。最終 UserOperation は Passkey で
確認します。MetaMask や Coinbase Wallet は任意です。」

## So there is no wallet sign-in to build, and that is the point

The previous three options -- give a wallet session its own kind, promote it to
the passkey's, or add wallet support to the hosted authorization server -- were
answers to a question the Coinbase model does not ask. In that model **the
passkey IS the wallet's owner**: the account address is derived from the
passkey's public key, so signing in with the passkey is what produces the
account. A second, wallet-shaped way in would be a WEAKER root for the same
identity, not an alternative one.

That is also exactly what the homepage means by 「a wallet signature alone
authorizes nothing」. It is not a limitation to work around; it is the design.

An external wallet (MetaMask, Coinbase Wallet) stays what
`/api/wallet/connect/*` already makes it -- something attached to an identity
that exists, which is why those routes require a human session.

## What was missing was the screen

`/wallet` in the CLI: three tabs. Account (address, owner kind, factory, entry
point, deployment state, whether the address is the same across chains, whether
a private key is stored), Chains (each supported chain and whether an owner
UserOperation can be sent on it), Capabilities (receive, propose-send,
sign-and-submit, add-owner).

Two words were chosen rather than reused: `private key 保存していません` instead
of running it through the same yes/no formatter as the capabilities, where
「できません」 reads as a missing feature when it is the opposite -- nothing is
stored because nothing needs to be. And the panel says `pending` means the
UserOperation path is not through yet, not that permission is missing.

## Measured

On a pty against the live resident, through the installed CLI: `/wallet` opens
on Account with the real address, `coinbase-smart-wallet 1.1`, entry point 0.6,
`not-yet-deployed`, same address across chains, no private key stored.

`not-yet-deployed` is not a fault to fix. A counterfactual account has an
address before it has code, and can receive at that address before it is
deployed; the panel says so.
"}]
